{"type": "thinking", "text": "Very interesting! Redo brought back line 5 AND reverted line 2 to \"20\" (not 200). So Undo had reverted the char deletion (200→?) plus removed line5; Redo re-applied line5 creation and char deletion. Hmm, confusing. States:\n- S0: line2=200, line5 exists. (before char delete)\n- S1: line2=20, line5 exists. (after char delete)\n- Undo → S0? But observed line2=200, line5 GONE (some other state S-1).\n- Redo → S1 (line2=20, line5 exists).\n\nSo Undo went to S-1 (a state before line5 creation with line2=200) and Redo restored S1 (after deletion). That means undo/redo stack is inconsistent: Undo skipped a state (S0) and went to S-1, while Redo went forward to S1. That's an undo/redo bug.\n\nHmm, wait: maybe the app's undo removed the *last line* (line 5) and reverted text to 200, i.e., Undo did two things. Then Redo restored both (line5 back, text 20). Actually Redo restoring text 20 is consistent with redo of the delete-char operation, plus line5? The redo also brought back line5 — which Undo removed. If Undo removed line5, Redo re-adds line5 and applies the char delete. That's consistent with Undo doing: [delete char undo → 200] and [remove line5]?? Odd.\n\nAlternatively, Undo reverted to a snapshot where line2=200 and line5 absent. That snapshot is the state BEFORE line5 was added? But at that time line2 was 200. Yes! S-1 = (line2=200, no line5). Undo jumped back two operations. Redo jumped forward two operations to S1 = (line2=20, line5). So the undo/redo stack is a list of snapshots and Undo went back 2? Hmm.\n\nMaybe the app's undo history records each *line* change: typing \"15% of 240\" into new line etc. Actually line5 creation consisted of: press Return (new line), type chars (15, %, space, of, 240). Those are many state changes! Undo might have undone *all* of those in one step (per \"calculation\"?) if the app coalesces per-line edits. If so, undo removed line5 entirely (that's one logical change) — fine. And it also reverted line2 from 20 back to 200? That is another logical change (the char delete). So Undo did the last two actions? Unless the char delete was undone as part of... hmm.\n\nCould it be that the app's \"Undo\" reverts to the last saved state? The app autosaves maybe after a debounce. Undo = restore last auto-saved snapshot? If the file was saved at (line2=200, line5) at some point... no, then undo would show line5 present.\n\nI'm over-analyzing. This might be a legitimate undo/redo bug worth reporting: Undo removed line 5 (a line unrelated to the last edit) and Redo did not restore the same state (it restored the deleted char). But is it in scope? The task says test reversal. This is an area worth reporting if reproducible.\n\nLet me try to reproduce cleanly with a controlled experiment in this file:\n\nState now: line2=20, line5 exists.\nPlan:\nA. Add a new line \"77\" (line6): press Return on line5? Actually focus? Let me tap line5 field then press Return → new line 6; type 77. Then observe (6 lines).\nB. Edit line2: change \"20\" to \"21\" (delete 1 char, type 1). Observe line count 6, line2=21.\nC. Tap Undo once. Observe: what changes? Ideally line2 back to 20, 6 lines remain. If instead a line disappears, that's the bug.\n\nLet me do it. Tap line5 field (element 13)."}
