{"type": "thinking", "text": "Cleaned up: nine original files.\n\nNow let me make a final pass on the remaining areas:\n- Percentages: maybe verify \"disc% of price\" error persists after app restart (yes, static data).\n- Check the temperature file after reopen (values unchanged).\n- Check totals after app restart (values unchanged).\n\nI've already reopened some. State persistence verified for the test file; presumably the original files unchanged.\n\nRemaining possible bugs I should investigate:\n1. The \"avg\" bug: confirmed. \"total\" including avg: same. \"grand_total\": logged.\n2. Temperature negative values: confirmed.\n3. Percentage variable forms: confirmed.\n4. Rename preselect: rejected twice (maybe not a bug per dev).\n\nLet me think whether there are other bugs in the \"creating and renaming files\" area:\n- Rename dialog title says \"Rename File\"; buttons Cancel/Rename; counter 8/50; rename works; cancel works; empty name disables Rename. Seems solid.\n- Creating: creates new \"Untitled\" file when content added; empty files discarded; persists; newest first. Seems solid.\n- One thing: after creating a new file, the first line's hint \"Type here...\"; typing through the *hardware/IME text* put text into the wrong row (line 1 vs focused line 2). But keyboard taps work. Could be the app's focus handling after Return: The observe said line 2 focused, yet type_text went to line1. If it's an app bug, typing via a real keyboard (Bluetooth) after pressing Return might append to the previous line! That would be a serious input bug. But the harness's type_text may not respect Android focus reliably. Since the on-screen keypad taps typed into line2 correctly, the app's focus is fine; the harness's text injection used the first EditText. I'd attribute it to the automation. Not reporting.\n\nHmm, wait: in the rename dialog, the automation's type_text inserted at position 0 (\"xUntitled\"), which is also weird. That suggests the harness's type_text commits text to the focused EditText but the cursor position may be off? In the dialog, after typing \"x\" via type_text, it became \"xUntitled\". If the harness uses `InputConnection.commitText` at the current cursor, and the cursor was at 0... why would it be at 0? The app might have set selection to 0 when the dialog opened (not select-all). That's plausible: the app might call `setSelection(0)` (start) instead of select-all! Then type_text inserted at 0. But the screenshot showed the caret at the END (after \"Untitled\")... Hmm, the screenshot's caret might have been the IME composing underline; let me re-examine: In the screenshot, \"Untitled\" had an underline beneath, and I thought there was a vertical bar after \"d\". The underline is the composing region. The caret could be at the start (before \"U\") hidden. So the app may indeed place the cursor at 0.\n\nThen the keypad \"x\" tap appended at the end (\"Untitledx\") — wait, keypad tap should insert at the caret (0) → \"xUntitled\". But we got \"Untitledx\" from the keypad. Contradiction! Unless the keypad tap first moved the cursor to the end? On Android, tapping a key doesn't move the cursor.\n\nHmm, unless the app's EditText selection was at the end for keypad taps. So type_text (IME commit) behaved differently (inserted at start) than keypad taps (appended at end). That indicates the harness's type_text uses the AccessibilityNodeInfo's `ACTION_SET_TEXT` or similar that replaces text from position 0? Actually, type_text(clear=false) might use `setText` semantics? If it appends? Hmm.\n\nGiven this, maybe the harness's type_text is unreliable for cursor position. But my keypad taps are reliable: they proved the name was NOT preselected (appended at end; DEL removed one char).\n\nSo the preselect finding stands.\n\nBut the dev rejected... Perhaps the dev's expected behavior is different or they couldn't reproduce because their device's IME behaves differently. I'll leave it.\n\nLet me do a final check of the temperatures: verify that after app restart values are still wrong (static file data). Yes.\n\nShould I check the Temperature sums for negative values? Not in the file. The area \"temperatures and units\" is covered.\n\nLet me also verify that the temperature error is not just a display of a wrong unit conversion for negative values but also that \"in fahrenheit\" works for positive values. Let me test \"25 celsius in fahrenheit\" in the scratchpad quickly to know the scope (positive conversion correct?). If positive in-fahrenheit is correct, the bug is only negative. Not needed, but a 2-call test. Let me skip; the confirmed report is enough.\n\nOne more consideration for percentages: The Percentages file line 3/4/6 show \"Err\". But do the results update if you fix the variable name? Not needed.\n\nNow let me think about whether the \"avg\" and \"grand_total\" are actually the same bug as \"total includes summary results\". The dev confirmed avg and said grand_total already logged. Good.\n\nAre there any other summary-related defects? What about `avg` when the line above is a `grand_total`? Same.\n\nWhat about `total` including the *blank line* reset behavior across sections: The changed note says \"gathered from every line above them\". In Monthly budget, line 6 total = 49.5 (only after blank). If the intended change is to include lines across blank separators, then the app fails to do that. Should I report? Hmm.\n\nThe precondition's Monthly budget: \"salary = 2500, bonus = 300.5, total, a blank line, gift = 49.5, total, grand_total\". If total at line 6 were supposed to include everything above (including line3 total), that'd be 5650.5, and grand_total...? The app shows line6 total=49.5 and grand_total=5700. The expected grand_total (2850) is consistent with section totals 2800.5 and 49.5. If instead line6 total=5650.5, grand_total would be some other weird value. The most sensible interpretation: sections separated by blank lines; line6 total = section sum. So the app's line6 total is correct.\n\nBut does the changed note \"gathered from every line above them\" mean the aggregation should include lines above the blank line within the same section? If the blank line is just a visual separator and total should gather every line above, then line6 total should include 2500+300.5+49.5 = 2850 (excluding the line3 total). The app gives 49.5. Which is intended? Hmm.\n\nThink about how a user would interpret `total` after a blank line: If I write a file with two sections separated by a blank line, I might expect `total` to sum only the current section (as the app does). The precondition designer included a blank line to test exactly this behavior. The expected grand_total 2850 implies grand_total combines the two totals. So the blank line is a section separator. I'll assume the app's behavior is intended.\n\nAlso, \"results they combine are now gathered from every line above them\" may refer to *within the section*, all lines above (not just until an empty expression). Hmm.\n\nGiven the dev confirmed the avg and grand_total bugs, but didn't flag total line6, I'll not report blank-line behavior.\n\nNow, one more area check: Did the build's \"percentage syntax new parsing rule for numbers\" introduce a regression for modulo? E.g., \"100 % 13\" works (Remainders ✓). \"19.5 % 4\" = 3.50 ✓. Good.\n\nCould \"disc% of price\" fail because variable percentage isn't supported at all, i.e., the precondition expects it to work (dev changed the parser to support percentage forms for numbers, not variables) — that's exactly the reported bug. Confirmed.\n\nLet me check if percentage with a *literal* and a variable base works: \"10% of price\" — the parser recognizes 10% and then \"of price\" looks up price. In the Percentages file, \"10% of 80\" works; \"tax% of 1200\" errors at \"tax%\". So a literal% of variable? Let me test \"10% of price\" in the scratchpad, but price isn't defined there. Could test in a file. Not necessary — the bug is variable%.\n\n